<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uchchala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1101–<a href="/years/1111">1111</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uchchala was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> who founded the second Lohara dynasty. With his brother Sussala he rebelled against the tyrannical King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, who was overthrown and killed in 1101. Uchchala reigned until 1111, when he was murdered in a conspiracy of nobles, and Sussala later succeeded him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Uchchala, also known as Uchchala Deva, was born in 1070, the eldest son of his parents and a close relative of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha-of-kashmir">Harsha of Kashmir</a>. With his brother Sussala he entered the valley and took a low-ranking command in the royal army, which was under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>'s control. By showing himself brave and loyal he became a privileged member of the royal court, but the king came to suspect the brothers of aiming at the throne and saw them as rivals to his son Bhoja. Uchchala married Jayamati, remembered as a beautiful queen fond of singing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1099, during a drought, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> imposed heavy taxes on the people. Uchchala resented them, and the brothers, fearing that they would be treated the same way, left the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a>. After a time away they returned with an army, attacked the king and burnt the capital; the king's son Bhoja was killed in the fighting. Uchchala then took the throne and is regarded as the founder of the second Lohara dynasty. He restored religious buildings and built new temples.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a>, the 12th-century historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Rajatarangini</a>, Sussala was among those who were ambitious for the throne. To forestall any move by his brother, Uchchala divided the kingdom into two parts and placed Sussala on the throne of the second, which Sussala ruled with his support. Uchchala was assassinated by his enemy Radda at Banihal on 8 December 1111, and Radda held power for one night only.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uchchala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
